    <h1 class="text-4xl font-bold mt-8 mb-8">Canada: PIPEDA & Provincial Laws</h1>

    <h2>Federal Law: PIPEDA</h2>
    <p>In Canada, the Personal Information Protection and Electronic Documents Act (PIPEDA) is the federal law that recognizes the validity of electronic signatures. It establishes that a signature requirement can be met by an electronic signature if it proves identity and intent.</p>

    <h2>Provincial Laws</h2>
    <p>Because property and civil rights fall under provincial jurisdiction, each province has its own electronic commerce act (e.g., Ontario's Electronic Commerce Act, 2000). These laws generally mirror the Uniform Electronic Commerce Act (UECA), ensuring broad acceptance across the country.</p>

    <p>As in the US and UK, standard business agreements, vendor contracts, and NDAs are perfectly suitable for execution via <a href="/">usignpdf</a> in Canada.</p>
